AU  - Kwon, Jeongyeol
AU  - Efroni, Yonathan
AU  - Caramanis, Constantine
AU  - Mannor, Shie
PY  - 2023
T2  - Proceedings of the 40th International Conference on Machine Learning
AB  - We consider episodic reinforcement learning in reward-mixing Markov decision processes (RMMDPs): at the beginning of every episode nature randomly picks a latent reward model among $M$ candidates and an agent interacts with the MDP throughout the episode for $H$ time steps. Our goal is to learn a near-optimal policy that nearly maximizes the $H$ time-step cumulative rewards in such a model. Prior work established an upper bound for RMMDPs with $M=2$. In this work, we resolve several open questions for the general RMMDP setting. We consider an arbitrary $M\ge2$ and provide a sample-efficient algorithm–$EM^2$–that outputs an $\epsilon$-optimal policy using $O \left(\epsilon^-2 \cdot S^d A^d \cdot poly(H, Z)^d \right)$ episodes, where $S, A$ are the number of states and actions respectively, $H$ is the time-horizon, $Z$ is the support size of reward distributions and $d=O(\min(M,H))$. We also provide a $(SA)^\Omega( M) / \epsilon^2$ lower bound, supporting that super-polynomial sample complexity in $M$ is necessary.
